<https://loze-ontology.org/isReferencePointIn> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "In the case of John being taller than Dan, Dan is the reference point; in the case of an object falling down from the top of a tower, the top of the tower is the reference point; in the case of a vehicle moving on the surface of Earth, Earth - or some Earth-based geolocation framework - is the reference point" .
<https://loze-ontology.org/NonHumanAnimal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In ordinary talk as well as legal talk, non-human animals are usually called just 'animals'" .
<https://loze-ontology.org/NonHumanAnimal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An animal that is not a specimen of the species Homo sapiens" .
<https://loze-ontology.org/hasCause> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and an eventuality e' if and only if e' is a cause of e" .
<https://loze-ontology.org/> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A top-level ontology largely inspired - with a few deviations and additions - by the four-category ontology outlined by the philosopher E. J. Lowe (2006), supplemented with the concept of a schematic particular explored by Eddy Zemach (1970)" .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The core participants in a place occupation are the place (:isHostIn) and the particular entity that occupies it (:isHostedIn)" .
_:n063a40871ee841d298f54ea38223eb81b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:n063a40871ee841d298f54ea38223eb81b12 .
